WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.417 --> 00:00:05.794
So a couple of years ago,

00:00:05.794 --> 00:00:07.837
I went through a pretty brutal breakup,

00:00:07.837 --> 00:00:09.297
and to keep myself distracted,

00:00:09.297 --> 00:00:12.300
I decided to set myself
a ridiculous art challenge:

00:00:12.342 --> 00:00:14.928
o create the closest thing
to a real-life time machine

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:16.763
with very little technical experience

00:00:16.763 --> 00:00:19.057
and the bank account
of a college graduate.

00:00:19.099 --> 00:00:21.476
Now, I know this sounds crazy,

00:00:21.518 --> 00:00:24.604
but avoiding your feelings
is a very powerful force.

00:00:24.646 --> 00:00:25.814
And I had a plan.

00:00:25.814 --> 00:00:27.524
You see, all I had to do

00:00:27.524 --> 00:00:30.652
was to spend an entire year
wearing 3D cameras on my face,

00:00:30.694 --> 00:00:33.530
record my life
in first person for 365 days,

00:00:33.572 --> 00:00:35.699
transfer that into a series of hard drives

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:37.909
and then build
a virtual reality simulation

00:00:37.951 --> 00:00:40.245
that will let me experience
those memories in 3D.

00:00:40.245 --> 00:00:42.163
That's all I had to do.

00:00:42.163 --> 00:00:45.083
In other words, I was going to create
a personal time machine,

00:00:45.125 --> 00:00:47.961
a virtual-reality invention
that will let me go back in time

00:00:47.961 --> 00:00:50.046
and experience moments from my past.

00:00:50.088 --> 00:00:52.632
And building this was pretty insane.

00:00:52.674 --> 00:00:56.094
I had to learn a lot of technical skills
in a really short span of time,

00:00:56.094 --> 00:00:59.347
and for an entire year,
my Tinder profile looked like this.

00:00:59.389 --> 00:01:02.934
(Laughter)

00:01:02.934 --> 00:01:04.644
But in the end, it was all worth it

00:01:04.644 --> 00:01:06.688
because the final result
was so much cooler

00:01:06.688 --> 00:01:08.231
than I could have ever imagined.

00:01:08.273 --> 00:01:09.441
So this is how it works.

00:01:09.482 --> 00:01:13.111
You put on a VR headset, you select a date
and time you’d like to go back to,

00:01:13.111 --> 00:01:16.156
you pull a lever and then, bam.

00:01:16.197 --> 00:01:19.159
I got to see exactly what I saw
at that given point in time.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:21.494
And in this case, I was eating
a bagel in a tree,

00:01:21.536 --> 00:01:23.371
which was the thing that I used to do.

00:01:23.371 --> 00:01:26.708
And now I could go back in time
and re-experience a past trip,

00:01:26.708 --> 00:01:28.460
try a meal again for the first time

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:31.755
and even hang out with a friend
who is no longer with us.

00:01:31.796 --> 00:01:34.382
And the longer I spent
with this time machine,

00:01:34.424 --> 00:01:37.218
the more I realized that I had
accidentally built something

00:01:37.218 --> 00:01:39.888
that was much more meaningful
than I initially imagined.

00:01:39.929 --> 00:01:43.767
Because this wasn't just an entirely new
way for you to experience your memories.

00:01:43.767 --> 00:01:46.853
This was an entirely new way
for you to understand yourself.

00:01:46.895 --> 00:01:48.355
As I used the time machine,

00:01:48.355 --> 00:01:51.483
I started to become hyper aware
of how much of our lives we forget

00:01:51.483 --> 00:01:52.651
over the years

00:01:52.692 --> 00:01:55.487
and how important it is for us
to enjoy the moments we have

00:01:55.487 --> 00:01:57.447
before our minds inevitably forget them.

00:01:57.447 --> 00:01:59.032
I learned to be kinder to myself,

00:01:59.032 --> 00:02:01.576
as watching myself wander
through life in third person

00:02:01.576 --> 00:02:03.995
kind of taught me how much
of a dick I am to myself

00:02:03.995 --> 00:02:05.205
when things get rough.

00:02:05.246 --> 00:02:09.167
And I even started to have
an active friendship with myself,

00:02:09.209 --> 00:02:12.337
as in many of my recordings,
you find me talking to future me,

00:02:12.379 --> 00:02:15.548
sending thoughts, hopes
and feelings across time.

00:02:16.132 --> 00:02:18.176
And this was really cool.

00:02:18.176 --> 00:02:20.595
And it was also really confusing.

00:02:20.595 --> 00:02:23.515
Because I was just trying
to get over my ex with some art.

00:02:23.556 --> 00:02:26.059
But somehow by mixing art and technology,

00:02:26.059 --> 00:02:28.436
we ended up in a really interesting place.

00:02:28.478 --> 00:02:29.771
And the world agreed.

00:02:29.813 --> 00:02:31.439
The time machine went super viral,

00:02:31.439 --> 00:02:32.732
50 million people saw it,

00:02:32.732 --> 00:02:35.402
and it even inspired people
to start entire companies

00:02:35.443 --> 00:02:37.028
based off this concept.

00:02:37.028 --> 00:02:40.240
So it seemed that by mixing art and tech,

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:42.325
I created something
innovative by accident.

00:02:42.367 --> 00:02:44.369
And because that made me feel pretty good,

00:02:44.411 --> 00:02:46.621
I was like, can we do that again?

00:02:47.038 --> 00:02:48.873
Could I just take emerging technology,

00:02:48.915 --> 00:02:52.168
you know, the stuff that's weird,
the stuff that we don't understand,

00:02:52.210 --> 00:02:54.212
do some art and just
accidentally innovate?

00:02:54.254 --> 00:02:56.256
And so our journey begins.

00:02:56.297 --> 00:02:58.717
A couple of months go by,
and ladies and gentlemen,

00:02:58.758 --> 00:02:59.884
I was back in the game.

00:02:59.926 --> 00:03:01.511
I had a new girlfriend.

00:03:01.553 --> 00:03:03.680
Her name was Max,
and things were going well,

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:05.890
and Valentine's Day
was just around the corner.

00:03:05.932 --> 00:03:09.894
So me being me, I decided to gift her
a little bit of an art project.

00:03:09.936 --> 00:03:13.690
So we're about to see is
what I can see from my perspective.

00:03:13.732 --> 00:03:16.776
I'm wearing an AR headset
that's tracking her body in real time.

00:03:16.776 --> 00:03:17.944
So here we go.

00:03:20.029 --> 00:03:22.615
So love is a really abstract feeling.

00:03:22.657 --> 00:03:25.410
It's something we all feel
but we can't see or hear.

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:28.663
So I created this experience
that turned the touch between two lovers

00:03:28.705 --> 00:03:31.875
into this visual and auditory symphony,

00:03:31.875 --> 00:03:35.795
basically transforming intimacy
into visuals and sounds,

00:03:35.795 --> 00:03:37.464
which I thought was pretty cool.

00:03:37.505 --> 00:03:40.800
And Max got to play me too,
which I think she liked.

00:03:40.842 --> 00:03:42.302
But when I posted this online,

00:03:42.302 --> 00:03:45.805
people started telling me that I had built
something completely different.

00:03:45.805 --> 00:03:47.140
And that is when I realized

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:50.101
that by engaging in this artistic
exploration of intimacy,

00:03:50.101 --> 00:03:52.228
I created a new kind
of musical instrument,

00:03:52.228 --> 00:03:56.065
a particle synthesizer that lets you play
complex sounds in midair like this.

00:03:56.107 --> 00:04:02.572
(Music)

00:04:08.161 --> 00:04:09.370
Pretty cool, right?

00:04:09.370 --> 00:04:12.165
(Applause)

00:04:12.165 --> 00:04:13.792
Wait, we're not done.

00:04:13.792 --> 00:04:15.001
Just for good measure,

00:04:15.043 --> 00:04:18.254
I took those instruments,
and I attached them to a piece of clothing

00:04:18.254 --> 00:04:21.257
so I could now play my own clothes
with my hand in mid-air,

00:04:21.299 --> 00:04:22.467
which is ridiculous.

00:04:22.509 --> 00:04:26.805
And I think it's the first example
of interactive digital musical fashion.

00:04:26.805 --> 00:04:29.057
So for better or for worse,

00:04:29.057 --> 00:04:31.518
art took us to some
really interesting ideas.

00:04:31.518 --> 00:04:34.103
And at this point, I thought
I was onto something.

00:04:34.145 --> 00:04:36.606
But then I had another breakup.

00:04:36.648 --> 00:04:38.316
Audience: Aww.

00:04:38.358 --> 00:04:41.611
LR: But it’s OK because an artist
can do what an artist does best,

00:04:41.611 --> 00:04:44.155
which is to turn heartbreak into art.

00:04:44.197 --> 00:04:47.992
So jokes aside, this was actually
a really difficult time in my life

00:04:47.992 --> 00:04:51.162
because not only I was going through
all of that emotional turmoil,

00:04:51.204 --> 00:04:53.331
I was also moving
to an entirely new country.

00:04:53.331 --> 00:04:55.124
I was leaving my hometown from Brazil

00:04:55.166 --> 00:04:58.545
and moving into the United States,
thousands of miles away from family,

00:04:58.545 --> 00:05:00.547
no friends, didn't know anybody.

00:05:00.588 --> 00:05:04.467
So it was probably the loneliest
I've been in my entire life.

00:05:04.926 --> 00:05:09.013
So why not use this opportunity to take
my misery and make something cool?

00:05:09.472 --> 00:05:11.933
What if we use VR
to create an experience

00:05:11.975 --> 00:05:15.144
that's specifically designed
to foster the connection that I needed

00:05:15.144 --> 00:05:17.063
to make me feel closer to strangers?

00:05:17.105 --> 00:05:19.774
And that was the genesis
of "Where Thoughts Go."

00:05:19.816 --> 00:05:21.150
So this is how it works.

00:05:21.192 --> 00:05:22.944
You enter these virtual worlds

00:05:22.944 --> 00:05:25.530
in which you're surrounded
by these sleeping creatures.

00:05:25.572 --> 00:05:28.366
Each one of them is a voice message
left by another user

00:05:28.408 --> 00:05:29.909
who was there before you were.

00:05:29.951 --> 00:05:32.203
If you raise your fingers
and touch them gently,

00:05:32.203 --> 00:05:35.540
you can listen to the voice messages
that people have left behind.

00:05:35.582 --> 00:05:37.125
And in each one of these worlds,

00:05:37.166 --> 00:05:39.460
you're asked a different
personal question.

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:40.920
So by playing this experience,

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:43.381
you're essentially going down
an intimacy funnel

00:05:43.423 --> 00:05:46.885
in which you're getting to learn
about deeply intimate aspects

00:05:46.926 --> 00:05:48.094
of other people's lives.

00:05:48.094 --> 00:05:50.638
And of course, at the end
of each one of these worlds,

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:54.100
you can put your hands together,
give birth to your own little creature,

00:05:54.100 --> 00:05:57.645
speak into it, and leave your messages
behind for others to find.

00:05:57.687 --> 00:05:59.939
It was sweet, beautiful, anonymous.

00:05:59.939 --> 00:06:02.108
And people would say things
in this experience

00:06:02.150 --> 00:06:04.611
that they wouldn't share
with their closest friends.

00:06:04.611 --> 00:06:05.904
And when I was making this,

00:06:05.945 --> 00:06:08.031
everyone told me it was a horrible idea.

00:06:08.448 --> 00:06:11.367
People would tell me
that trolls would destroy the entire app,

00:06:11.409 --> 00:06:13.328
that people would be mean to each other,

00:06:13.328 --> 00:06:14.829
they would make each other cry.

00:06:14.871 --> 00:06:16.039
And you know what?

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:17.624
They weren't wrong.

00:06:17.665 --> 00:06:20.293
(Laughter)

00:06:20.919 --> 00:06:23.379
So when I started testing
"Where Thoughts Go"

00:06:23.421 --> 00:06:25.298
with the first couple of hundred users,

00:06:25.298 --> 00:06:27.300
I found out that roughly
20 percent of them

00:06:27.342 --> 00:06:29.177
were crying at least once per session.

00:06:29.177 --> 00:06:32.513
This is what I like to call
"Where Thoughts Go's" cry rate.

00:06:32.513 --> 00:06:35.266
But people weren't crying
because they were being bullied.

00:06:35.266 --> 00:06:38.227
People were crying because they were
feeling emotionally moved

00:06:38.269 --> 00:06:39.520
by the messages of others.

00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:43.232
We had created a system that instilled
a deep sense of empathy and connection

00:06:43.232 --> 00:06:45.109
in a really short span of time.

00:06:45.151 --> 00:06:48.988
And by making this, not only I was able
to get the connection that I was craving,

00:06:48.988 --> 00:06:51.324
but I was able to give it
to others as well.

00:06:51.324 --> 00:06:53.576
And because of how
this experience was designed,

00:06:53.618 --> 00:06:55.912
there were virtually zero
trolls in the system.

00:06:55.912 --> 00:06:57.997
It's like people
were walking into a church

00:06:57.997 --> 00:07:00.291
and instinctively knew
to lower their voices,

00:07:00.333 --> 00:07:01.918
treat each other respectfully

00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:04.420
and treat the space
they were in as sacred,

00:07:04.420 --> 00:07:06.506
which is a cool thing
you can only do in VR.

00:07:06.506 --> 00:07:08.591
And when I put things that way,

00:07:08.633 --> 00:07:11.052
it's clear that there's something
interesting here.

00:07:11.052 --> 00:07:13.513
Some innovative ideas that could be used,

00:07:13.554 --> 00:07:16.474
designs and lessons and approach
that could be applied

00:07:16.516 --> 00:07:17.892
towards the entire internet.

00:07:17.934 --> 00:07:20.019
But at this point,
you might go like, Lucas,

00:07:20.019 --> 00:07:21.813
you're not really innovating anymore.

00:07:21.813 --> 00:07:23.606
There's a lot of thought behind this.

00:07:23.606 --> 00:07:26.317
And yes, but no, because a year
after I launched this,

00:07:26.359 --> 00:07:30.738
medical doctors contacted me and told me
I invented a new form of group therapy.

00:07:30.738 --> 00:07:34.534
And now this art piece
is being used in a medical trial

00:07:34.534 --> 00:07:36.077
as an anxiety treatment tool.

00:07:36.077 --> 00:07:37.328
And shortly after that,

00:07:37.328 --> 00:07:39.956
I got to collaborate
with SpaceX and St Jude's

00:07:39.998 --> 00:07:42.458
to make a version of this
that astronauts could use

00:07:42.500 --> 00:07:45.169
so they could emotionally connect
with kids on Earth.

00:07:45.169 --> 00:07:47.755
This is not what I was trying to do.

00:07:47.755 --> 00:07:49.298
(Laughter)

00:07:49.340 --> 00:07:55.638
(Applause)

00:07:57.181 --> 00:07:59.142
But that's when it started to make sense.

00:07:59.183 --> 00:08:00.351
And it's really simple.

00:08:00.393 --> 00:08:02.687
When you take technology
that we don't understand

00:08:02.729 --> 00:08:04.313
and you approach it as an artist,

00:08:04.313 --> 00:08:07.734
you do things that the conventional
engineer would never think of doing.

00:08:07.775 --> 00:08:11.446
And when you do that in a space brimming
with possibility, like emerging tech,

00:08:11.446 --> 00:08:14.866
it's only a matter of time before you
stumble upon something of value.

00:08:14.907 --> 00:08:18.327
That is why mixing art and tech
is a great way to accidentally innovate.

00:08:18.327 --> 00:08:21.706
It takes you off the beaten path,
it removes creative constraints,

00:08:21.748 --> 00:08:25.168
and it allows you to approach technology
in a way that's unique to you,

00:08:25.168 --> 00:08:27.879
that leverages your passions
and your interests

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:30.465
and your life events and breakups.

00:08:30.506 --> 00:08:34.135
And all of this just comes together
and gives you a unique angle of approach.

00:08:34.135 --> 00:08:36.012
And that unique angle is everything.

00:08:36.012 --> 00:08:38.431
Because here's the thing
about emerging technology,

00:08:38.431 --> 00:08:42.351
and this applies to everything, VR, AI,
brain computers and whatever’s next.

00:08:42.727 --> 00:08:44.771
No one knows what they're doing.

00:08:44.812 --> 00:08:47.523
Everyone is just following blind hunches.

00:08:47.565 --> 00:08:49.692
It's too early
in a technology's life cycle

00:08:49.734 --> 00:08:52.695
for anyone to have a comprehensive view
of what it's good for.

00:08:52.737 --> 00:08:55.239
And in a world where no one
knows what they're doing,

00:08:55.239 --> 00:08:58.493
just following your gut
is a really great thing to do.

00:08:58.534 --> 00:09:02.538
Because what we have here
is unlimited creative potential.

00:09:02.580 --> 00:09:05.792
It's the ability to create
impossible objects out of thin air

00:09:05.833 --> 00:09:07.126
at no carbon costs,

00:09:07.168 --> 00:09:10.296
the ability to connect people
in ways never possible before.

00:09:10.338 --> 00:09:14.092
And it's the ability to create technology
that changes how we look at ourselves

00:09:14.092 --> 00:09:16.010
and the world around us.

00:09:16.052 --> 00:09:18.387
We have all the tools we need
to create a future

00:09:18.429 --> 00:09:20.348
that's beautiful and human and exciting.

00:09:20.348 --> 00:09:22.225
And the only thing that threatens this

00:09:22.266 --> 00:09:26.687
is if we have a massive
lapse of imagination.

00:09:27.146 --> 00:09:28.314
So ...

00:09:29.148 --> 00:09:33.319
Today I’m working on a new
project called “Pillow,”

00:09:33.361 --> 00:09:37.240
and I'm trying to turn your bed,
the most comfortable place in your home,

00:09:37.281 --> 00:09:42.203
into a mixed reality playground
of wellness, play and human connection.

00:09:42.245 --> 00:09:46.499
And this is an early prototype,
but look how beautiful this is.

00:09:46.499 --> 00:09:49.043
We're using mixed reality
to connect two beds together

00:09:49.043 --> 00:09:52.713
and give people a way to engage
that's never been possible before.

00:09:53.172 --> 00:09:54.674
And even though this is early,

00:09:54.715 --> 00:09:56.425
there's so much that we're learning

00:09:56.425 --> 00:09:58.553
about how to create meaningful connections

00:09:58.594 --> 00:10:00.888
between people and virtual environments,

00:10:00.930 --> 00:10:04.392
how to use artificial intelligence
to create immersive experiences

00:10:04.392 --> 00:10:05.893
that really feel personal

00:10:05.893 --> 00:10:09.188
and how to approach products
with accessibility in mind

00:10:09.230 --> 00:10:10.398
from the get-go.

00:10:10.398 --> 00:10:12.817
Things that work just as well
lying down on a couch

00:10:12.817 --> 00:10:14.694
as they do in a hospital bed.

00:10:14.694 --> 00:10:16.696
And I don't know where
this is going to go,

00:10:16.737 --> 00:10:18.739
but it's going to be
somewhere interesting.

00:10:18.781 --> 00:10:20.950
And when I look at this,
I just don't see art.

00:10:20.992 --> 00:10:22.160
I see research.

00:10:22.201 --> 00:10:25.121
I see things that could be turned
into products and companies.

00:10:25.121 --> 00:10:27.540
And I want more artists
to start tech companies.

00:10:27.582 --> 00:10:29.792
We need more artists in technology,

00:10:29.792 --> 00:10:34.088
but we also just need more technologists
to think a little bit like artists.

00:10:34.964 --> 00:10:37.633
Because some of the world's
most important ideas,

00:10:37.633 --> 00:10:39.260
you really can't stumble upon them

00:10:39.302 --> 00:10:41.512
by just trying to chase
the highest valuation.

00:10:41.512 --> 00:10:43.472
Sometimes you only stumble upon them

00:10:43.472 --> 00:10:46.017
when you approach technology
from a place of truth.

00:10:46.017 --> 00:10:49.353
When you build things just
because you need to make them

00:10:49.395 --> 00:10:52.523
and you don't know where you're going,
but if you follow your gut,

00:10:52.523 --> 00:10:53.649
you will be rewarded.

00:10:53.691 --> 00:10:55.610
And I don't know how or when,

00:10:55.610 --> 00:10:58.196
like with anything else
that I told you about today,

00:10:58.237 --> 00:10:59.530
but you will be rewarded.

00:10:59.530 --> 00:11:01.157
We will all be rewarded.

00:11:02.074 --> 00:11:05.286
And being true to yourself is just
a really good long-term strategy.

00:11:05.328 --> 00:11:08.915
Technology gives us the tools,
but art shows us the way.

00:11:08.956 --> 00:11:10.499
It's always been like this.

00:11:10.499 --> 00:11:12.668
It's hard to find
a single futuristic startup

00:11:12.710 --> 00:11:16.255
that hasn't been deeply inspired
by some kind of science fiction material.

00:11:16.255 --> 00:11:19.091
Engineers have always followed
the imaginations of artists,

00:11:19.133 --> 00:11:21.219
and that's not going
to change anytime soon.

00:11:21.260 --> 00:11:25.514
But what is changing is that technology
is becoming easier to use.

00:11:25.514 --> 00:11:27.934
I didn't know how to do
any of this five years ago.

00:11:27.975 --> 00:11:30.519
Artists don't have to be
in the background anymore.

00:11:30.561 --> 00:11:34.106
We can get our hands dirty
and build science nonfiction instead

00:11:34.106 --> 00:11:35.733
to create projects and companies

00:11:35.775 --> 00:11:38.569
that challenge the engineer-dominated
visions of technology

00:11:38.569 --> 00:11:39.820
and propose something new,

00:11:39.820 --> 00:11:42.490
something a little weirder
with a little more heart.

00:11:42.490 --> 00:11:45.409
And I think in the process,
we can unlock new realities

00:11:45.451 --> 00:11:47.370
that not only we are excited to live in,

00:11:47.370 --> 00:11:49.914
but that we're excited to see
our children grow up in.

00:11:49.956 --> 00:11:54.794
For the longest time in technology
artists imagine so engineers can build.

00:11:54.794 --> 00:11:57.755
But I think engineers build,
so artists can imagine.

00:11:58.172 --> 00:12:01.175
So if you have a crazy project
in your back pocket,

00:12:01.217 --> 00:12:03.928
something you always wanted to do
but never got around to,

00:12:03.970 --> 00:12:05.179
you should go for it.

00:12:05.221 --> 00:12:08.891
You will never know what
you're going to find on the other side.

00:12:08.933 --> 00:12:10.101
Thank you.

00:12:10.142 --> 00:12:17.024
(Applause and cheers)

